    "why": "Inside a case the other variables are still free, so the case is proved only when the expression reduces to something true for every value of the remaining variables.",
    "counterexample": "In case p = true, p -> (q -> (p and q)) reduces to q -> q, which is true for both values of q - that is what makes the case work. If it had reduced to q, the case would not be settled."
